---
import headshot from '../assets/headshot.jpg';
import PortraitLayout from "../components/layout/PortraitLayout.astro";
import { getEntry } from "astro:content";
import { isPublished } from "@/utils/posts";

const pageTitle = "About Me";
const pageDescription =
  "Andrew Tarrh — software engineer focused on data pipelines, infrastructure, and privacy-minded systems for mission-driven work.";

// Link the civic post only once it is live, so About can ship before it.
const civicPost = await getEntry("blog", "showing-up-online-and-in-person");
const showCivicPost = civicPost !== undefined && isPublished(civicPost);

---
<PortraitLayout 
	title={pageTitle} 
	description={pageDescription} 
	portraitImage={headshot}>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      I’m Andrew Tarrh — a software engineer with a backend / data-engineering bent.
      I’ve spent my career building and operating data pipelines and cloud-native
      systems, with a particular focus on reliability, privacy, and cost-conscious
      design.
    </p>

    <p>
      Earlier in my career I worked on Amazon Alexa’s Machine Learning Data Platform,
      where I helped maintain large-scale ETL workflows and the operational tooling
      behind them. That environment shaped how I work: I care about clear ownership,
      pragmatic instrumentation, and making systems easier (and safer) to operate.
      It also made me AI-forward early on. Today I use AI tools throughout my own
      development workflow, and they’ve changed how quickly I can pick up a new stack.
    </p>

    <p>
      After Amazon I stepped back from full-time roles and took on independent contract
      work, including evaluating LLM code generation and mentoring engineers on data
      infrastructure. I also put a lot of time into building out a van, which is still
      a work in progress (and a future blog topic). Along the way I spent about six
      months volunteering with <strong>WeVote</strong>, a nonpartisan civic-tech
      nonprofit, contributing UI components and bug fixes to its Node/React/Flux web app
      and improvements to its Python/Django API server.
    </p>

    <p>
      Since April 2026 I’ve worked on the Platform team at
      <strong>Arizona State University’s Learning Enterprise</strong>, contracting through
      Dexian. We build and modernize the backend systems and data integrations behind
      ASU’s enterprise applications and digital learning platforms, like Salesforce and
      Canvas. Lately I’ve focused on data consistency: making sure records agree as they
      move between systems. The work is fully remote, with a few in-person meetups a year,
      which suits me well.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you're interested in my professional experience, you can download a PDF version 
      of my resume <a href="/andrew-tarrh-resume.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>What I’m doing now</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Working on data integrations and cross-system consistency for ASU Learning
        Enterprise’s Platform team.
      </li>
      <li>
        Volunteering with <strong>Voter Choice Arizona</strong>, a nonpartisan group that
        educates voters about ranked choice voting. In Tucson I collect signatures, talk
        with people at community events, and do some public speaking.
        {showCivicPost && (
          <>
            I wrote about it, and about my time with WeVote, in
            <a href="/blog/showing-up-online-and-in-person/">Showing Up, Online and in Person</a>.
          </>
        )}
      </li>
      <li>
        Writing here more often — about infrastructure, civic tech, and whatever else I’m
        learning.
      </li>
      <li>
        Chipping away at the van build.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What I care about working on</h2>
    <p>
      I’m happiest helping a small-to-mid-sized team build dependable data
      infrastructure: pipelines, integrations, observability, and the patterns that keep
      systems secure and maintainable. I’m drawn to organizations that take privacy
      seriously and work on public-benefit problems like education, health, and climate.
      Green energy is a long-standing interest — my master’s capstone forecast energy
      use for university buildings.
    </p>

    <p>
      I’m based in <strong>Tucson, Arizona</strong>, and I work remotely.
    </p>

    <h2>A note on values</h2>
    <p>
      I care a lot about community, civic engagement, sustainability, and housing
      justice. I’m interested in models like community land trusts and co-housing, and
      I try to make career choices that balance practical needs with long-term alignment.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <p class="muted">
      Want to reach out? Head to the <a href="/contact">contact page</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

</PortraitLayout>